" (https://tanvirarafin.github.io/assets/pdf/DATE_2026.pdf)\nciteturn7view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://tanvirarafin.github.io/assets/pdf/DATE_2026.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 705\nL0@P0: Authors Copy DO NOT DISTRIBUTE ©IEEE\nL1@P0: GPU Acceleration of the Sum-Check Protocol Over\nL2@P0: Towers of Binary Fields for Verifiable Computing\nL3@P0: Andrew Fan∗\nL4@P0: Palo Alto High School &\nL5@P0: George Mason University\nL6@P0: Fairfax, US\nL7@P0: afan4@gmu.edu\nL8@P0: Yanze Wu\nL9@P0: Cyber Security Engineering Department\nL10@P0: George Mason University\nL11@P0: Fairfax, US\nL12@P0: ywu42@gmu.edu\nL13@P0: Harry Han∗\nL14@P0: Thomas Jefferson High School for Science and Technology &\nL15@P0: George Mason University\nL16@P0: Fairfax, US\nL17@P0: harryhjhan@gmail.com\nL18@P0: Md Tanvir Arafin\nL19@P0: Cyber Security Engineering Department\nL20@P0: George Mason University\nL21@P0: Fairfax, US\nL22@P0: marafin@gmu.edu\nL23@P0: Abstract—Emerging zero-knowledge proof protocols such as\nL24@P0: Binius and Binius-FRI operate over towers of binary fields,\nL25@P0: allowing for ultra-fast polynomial commitments over a base field.\nL26@P0: Sum-check, a key protocol in algebraic proof systems, is one\nL27@P0: of the key implementation bottlenecks for Binius and similar\nL28@P0: protocols. While sum-check is a massively parallel algorithm, GPU\nL29@P0: acceleration of sum-check has received little attention due to the\nL30@P0: lack of native GPU support for binary field multiplication. Hence,\nL31@P0: in this paper, we explore the key issues in existing GPU-based\nL32@P0: sum-check accelerators and present SumCATS - an efficient GPU\nL33@P0: implementation for sum-check acceleration. SumCATS leverages\nL34@P0: two fundamental improvements over the existing solutions. First,\nL35@P0: it adapts a CPU-based algorithmic improvement to sum-check\nL36@P0: proving and applies it to GPUs by recognizing the reduction\nL37@P0: pattern and shared memory optimizations. Secondly, SumCATS\nL38@P0: reduces the number of global memory accesses by precomputing\nL39@P0: products of random challenges and using base field operations\nL40@P0: to reconstruct extension field elements. When these optimizations\nL41@P0: are combined, SumCATS achieves a significant speedup (1.81×\nL42@P0: on NVIDIA RTX 3090 Ti, 1.62× on NVIDIA A100) over the\nL43@P0: baseline GPU implementation (Binius-GPU) for sum-check over\nL44@P0: binary tower fields. The code and research artifacts for SumCATS\nL45@P0: design are available at https://github.com/SPIRE-GMU/sum cats.\nL46@P0: Index Terms—GPU Acceleration, Binary Fields, Sum-Check\nL47@P0: I. INTRODUCTION\nL48@P0: SNARKs (Succinct Non-interactive ARgument of Knowl\u0002edge) are a type of cryptographic protocol that allows a prover\nL49@P0: to prove their knowledge of some witness that satisfies a\nL50@P0: certain property. SNARKs often allow the prover to do this\nL51@P0: without revealing information about the witness itself, and\nL52@P0: allow for very short (succinct) transactions. Most SNARK\nL53@P0: designs combine sum-check or other polynomial Interactive\nL54@P0: Oracle Proof (IOP) protocols with a polynomial commitment\nL55@P0: scheme to generate an interactive proof protocol, and then apply\nL56@P0: the Fiat-Shamir heuristic [1] to make them non-interactive.\nL57@P0: ∗This work was conducted at George Mason University, where Andrew Fan\nL58@P0: and Harry Han were a part of the Aspiring Scientists’ Summer Internship\nL59@P0: Program.\nL60@P0: The sum-check protocol [2] is a fundamental building block\nL61@P0: in the design of many modern SNARKs such as Binius [3],\nL62@P0: [4]. It provides an efficient way for a prover to prove the sum\nL63@P0: of a polynomial without revealing the entire polynomial. Sum\u0002check, through a recursive algorithm, reduces a claim to the\nL64@P0: evaluation of the polynomial at a single random point in the\nL65@P0: field. The commitment step of such SNARK protocols is fast,\nL66@P0: leaving sum-check as the main bottleneck in the operation.\nL67@P0: Algorithms for accelerating the sum-check protocol have\nL68@P0: been explored thoroughly [2], [5], [6]. However, using GPUs\nL69@P0: for sum-check presents a fundamental challenge. For binary\nL70@P0: tower fields, addition is defined using bitwise XOR, but there\nL71@P0: is no efficient hardware implementation for multiplication. Ad\u0002ditionally, modern GPUs lack built-in instructions for algebraic\nL72@P0: number theory-based computations. These factors limit the\nL73@P0: speedup of applying GPUs to sum-check over a tower of binary\nL74@P0: fields. Hence, in this paper, we present a high-performance\nL75@P0: GPU implementation of the sum-check protocol over binary\nL76@P0: fields. Our key contributions are:\nL77@P0: • We draw inspiration from previous algorithmic improve\u0002ments [7] to build SumCATS: two GPU-based sum\u0002check accelerators. The first algorithmic improvement\nL78@P0: does more base field operations before introducing exten\u0002sion field multiplications each round. The second improve\u0002ment leverages fast base-extension field multiplications to\nL79@P0: reconstruct field elements on GPU threads, thereby signif\u0002icantly reducing the number of global memory accesses\nL80@P0: and enhancing the throughput of faster local memory.\nL81@P0: • We also reorganize the storage of field elements to take\nL82@P0: advantage of faster shared memory and the highly efficient\nL83@P0: parallel reduction pattern.\nL84@P0: • We implement SumCATS using the CUDA framework. On\nL85@P0: the NVIDIA RTX 3090 Ti, we achieve a speedup of 1.81×\nL86@P0: on the largest benchmark test case. On the NVIDIA A100,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsum_cats/sumcheck/binius-gpu at main · SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats · GitHub (https://github.com/SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats/tr